  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "L1 gold-context records report central oral-bioavailability estimates at or above 20% for the transferable training contexts, including absolute measurements (cilomilast 96%, moxifloxacin 91%, ketanserin 49–60%, 5-HTP 49%) and systemic-availability estimates (norfloxacin mean 40%, rufinamide 50–85%, doxifluridine ~50–80%, sulpiride ~35%, bevantolol 57%).",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C02","C03","C04","C05","C06","C07","C08","C09","C10","C11","C12","C13","C14","C15","C16","C17"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L2 corroborating records from the same contexts are consistent with F≥20% (cilomilast tablet 103%, moxifloxacin capsule F=0.971 and tablet 86%, bevantolol ~60%, doxifluridine 34–47% and 50–80%, 5-HTP 40%, rufinamide food-effect increase).",
      "card_ids": ["C18","C19","C20","C21","C22","C23","C24","C27","C28"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 oral exposure records show measurable human Cmax/AUC after oral dosing for carboxylic-acid-containing analogs (febuxostat, pefloxacin, elvitegravir, indomethacin), consistent with oral absorption; none provides affirmative evidence of F<20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C29","C30","C31","C32","C33","C34","C35","C36","C37","C38","C39","C40"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 gut-wall efflux/permeability data are mixed: a zwitterionic uracil-dione carboxylic acid analog shows very low Caco-2 Papp and high efflux ratio (C59) with saturable efflux in a close analog (C58), but other uracil-dione and carboxylic-acid analogs show moderate A-B permeability without strong efflux (C55/C60, C63/C64, C57/C56, C62/C54).",
      "card_ids": ["C59","C58","C55","C60","C63","C64","C57","C56","C62","C54"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The L5 efflux data for related uracil-dione acids (C59, C58) reinforce the physicochemical absorption concern for the query's ionized-acid, high-TPSA profile, but they are not query-specific measurements and do not affirmatively establish F<20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C59","C58"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "contradictory",
      "decision_effect": "weakened",
      "card_ids": ["C53","C54","C55","C56","C57","C58","C59","C60","C61","C62","C63","C64"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No query-specific absolute oral bioavailability, matched oral-versus-IV, or human PK data exist.",
    "No query-specific Caco-2 permeability, efflux-ratio, or intestinal-metabolism measurement is available; L5 efflux data are for analogs with different ionizable side chains.",
    "Query dose, formulation, and prandial state are unspecified; several training records show dose/food dependence.",
    "The strongest L5 negative record (C59) is for a zwitterionic uracil-dione with an amino-acid side chain, limiting transfer to the query's fused-thiophene acid.",
    "Physicochemical liabilities (logD -1.11, TPSA 119.85, full ionization) create uncertainty but are not affirmative evidence for F<20%."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Direct L1/L2 oral-bioavailability records for the transferable training contexts uniformly support F≥20%, and L3 oral exposure is consistent; L5 adds mixed mechanistic efflux/permeability data, including strong efflux for a zwitterionic uracil-dione acid analog, but this is not query-specific and does not overturn the direct endpoint evidence. Prediction remains high, with confidence lowered to low because of the unfavorable ionized-acid physicochemical profile and the new efflux concern."
}

